<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aimoré</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Botocudo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aimoré were Macro-Jê-speaking peoples of the Atlantic forest of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>, Espírito Santo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, later called Botocudos by the Portuguese for their lip and ear plugs. From the mid-16th century their raids devastated the captaincies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilheus">Ilhéus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> Seguro. They were attacked in official wars into the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aimore.jpg" alt="Aimoré" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ferrario, Giulio (1767-1847) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Botocudos-Chief-Brasil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Aimoré are one of several peoples of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> whom the Portuguese called Botocudo, from botoque, a plug; the others were the Xokleng and the Xeta. Some called themselves Nac-nanuk or Nac-poruk, meaning sons of the soil. Their original territory lay in Espírito Santo and reached inland to the headwaters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> (Belmonte) and the Doce River on the eastern slopes of the Espinhaço Mountains. They were made up of dozens of tribes speaking dialects or closely related languages, among them the Naknianuk, the Nakrehê and the Gutucrac of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the Portuguese adventurer Vasco Fernando Coutinho reached the coast in 1535, he built a fort at the head of Espírito Santo Bay to defend himself against the Aimorés and other peoples. The Aimoré War began in 1555, when the Aimorés answered the widespread enslavement of their people. Colonists drove them westward beyond the Serra dos Aimorés into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, where at the end of the 18th century they clashed with Europeans drawn by the diamond fields.</p><p>During the frontier wars of 1790 to 1820 every effort was made to destroy them: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> was deliberately spread among them and poisoned food was left in the forests, and one Portuguese commander boasted of having killed or ordered killed many hundreds. Paul Ehrenreich put their number at 5,000 in 1884, and in April 1939 only 68 Botocudo were alive in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. The Krenak, whose name joins kren, head, and nak, land, are the last Aimoré group to keep their language; in 2010 there were 350 Krenak in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Botocudos were nomadic hunter-gatherers of the forest. Their tools and utensils were of wood, their weapons reed spears and bows and arrows, and their shelters of leaf and bast seldom more than 1.2 metres high. They attributed the blessings of life to the Day Fire, the Sun, and evil to the Night Fire, the Moon, kept fires burning at graves to scare away evil spirits, and shot arrows into the sky during storms and eclipses. Their best-known mark was the disk of light barriguda wood worn in the lower lip, begun as early as the eighth year and enlarged until it could reach 10 cm across, together with ear plugs that stretched the lobes to the shoulders.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aimoré” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the mid-16th century repeated attacks by the <b>Aimoré</b> people destroyed mills and drove settlers away, and the population remained small.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-ilheus">Captaincy of Ilhéus</a></li><li><blockquote>Attacks by the <b>Aimoré</b> people repeatedly devastated settlements, and Jesuit missions were founded among indigenous groups.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-porto-seguro">Captaincy of Porto Seguro</a></li></ul></section>
